  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "A filibuster is a U.S. Senate procedural tactic used by a minority party to delay or block a vote on legislation by extending debate, requiring a 60-vote supermajority—known as cloture—to end debate and proceed to a vote. Primarily used to protect minority rights, modern filibusters often stop legislation without requiring long speeches. Key Aspects of the Filibuster Purpose: To prevent the majority from passing legislation, often forcing compromise or stalling action on controversial issues. How it Works: While historically involving long speeches (\"talking filibuster\"), the modern \"stealth\" filibuster involves a senator threatening to filibuster, forcing the Senate to secure 60 votes to invoke cloture. Rules & Cloture: Senate rules require a cloture motion (3/5ths of all senators, typically 60) to end debate. If the motion fails, the bill is stalled. Exceptions: Budget reconciliation bills, many executive branch nominations, and federal judicial appointments cannot be filibustered, allowing them to pass with a simple majority. Longest Filibuster: Senator Strom Thurmond set the record in 1957 by speaking for 24 hours and 18 minutes against the Civil Rights Act, according to Wikipedia. History: Emerging in the 1850s from a desire for unlimited debate, the rules were tightened in 1917 (introducing cloture) and later changed to 60 votes in 1975, as noted in U.S. Senate (.gov) records.",
